tainly an issue uh within the  confines of local jails you know so i'm  surprised by the fact that bond was  denied and he was you know sent to jail  uh pending trial or pending a mental  health evaluation yeah as a public  defender for almost seven years now i've  never seen someone's mental health get  better in jail so that's an odd thing  for me to see and jeannette what about  the 4.3 million dollars from gloria  satterfield's death where's that money  well it's really interesting after court  eric bland one of the attorneys for the  satterfield family said that they've  actually gotten some of that money back  and he said we will get all of it back  so i don't know how he thinks they're  going to get it unless it's an account  somewhere or they think that alec  murdoch might have some of this money  just kind of laying around i don't know  but they are confident they said that  they will get that money for their  clients all right let's hope that  happens very soon thank you all coming  up on law and crime daily convicting  killer derek chovin to appeal his case  with a new attorney plus will former  trump advisor steve bannon be held in  contempt of court when the select  committee will make their vote next on  longtime  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily the  so-called january 6 committee is set to  vote tuesday night whether to hold  former white house adviser steve bannon  in contempt of court meanwhile former  president donald trump files a lawsuit  to prevent the committee from accessing  his records  all this is in relation to the capitol  riots that happened on january 6th as  law and crime daily reported the house  select committee in charge of  investigating the january 6 attacks  requested bannon provide details about  conversations he had with former  president donald trump they say those  conversations happened leading up to the  riots bannon refused to provide the  information and didn't show up after  being subpoenaed on monday former  president trump filed a lawsuit to stop  the january 6 committee from attaining  his records he says the requests are  unconstitutionally overbroad and violate  the separation of powers principles this  comes after president joe biden refused  to back trump's claim that the records  are protected by executive privilege  in response to former president trump's  lawsuit the committee issued a statement  saying in part quote the former  president's clear objection is to stop  the select committee from getting to the  facts about january 6 and his lawsuit is  nothing more than an attempt to delay  and obstruct our probe  let's bring back defense attorney  matthew mengino and co-host terry austin  terry do you see any strengths or merit  in the former president's lawsuit to  stop the subpoena from banning  i absolutely think this is a long shot  as far as this lawsuit is concerned the  committee is requesting these documents  and it is an executive privilege whether  or not they should be produced biden has  said he's not going to assert this  executive privilege and he cited the  fact that there is a public interest to  see these documents and frankly i think  that these white house records should be  released and that the privilege does not  apply to trump who is a former president  and not a current executive so  you know trump is claiming clearly that  there is no legislative purpose for this  that it's a fishing expedition but i  think that this privilege is something  that is reserved for the executive and  his argument will not prevail at the end  of the day  now matthew does congress have the power  to vote on contempt charges before the  lawsuit is decided and if they do how do  you think they'll rule  well i think that  the congress does have the ability to  vote on contempt before this lawsuit is  resolved the lawsuit is not in any way  uh aimed at uh you know  declaring some sort of injunctive relief  and i think it would be a  separation of powers issue if it was you  can't thumb your nose at congress uh if  congress issues a subpoena if they want  records if they want you to appear you  just can't ignore them and  and i think that the congress is going  to take action in this case because you  simply  cannot let someone uh just turn their  back and and refuse to comply uh with  the authority of the united states  congress it's a dangerous precedent to  do nothing here and i think congress  will act yeah that makes sense i think i  think that's the line there a dangerous  precedent for them not to act i think  they will do so just so other people  don't get a similar idea great point  matthew turning now to chief  investigative correspondent brian ross  whose weekly episode of brian ross  investigates focuses on a little-known  military scandal brian  thanks brian coming up this week i'm  brian ross investigates a huge scandal  that most people have never heard of  involving the u.s navy  top admirals and a man by the name of  fat letter who used hookers and cash and  his own charm to win millions of dollars  in navy contracts across the pacific fat  leonard has pleaded guilty to corrupting  the u.s navy and creating a mafia-like  organization inside the navy partly  what's going on here is he believes  there's been a cover-up in the u.s navy  and a lot of the information that he  gave to investigators about admirals  that dealt with him wasn't acted upon in  his view and that's one of the reasons  that he talked that's coming up this  week on brian ross investigates on the  long crime trial network brian  thanks brian you can catch brian ross  investigates on tuesdays and thursdays  at 5 30 and 10 30 eastern time  when we come back derek chovin finds new  representation in court how private  attorneys stepped up to represent the  convicted murderer as he looks to appeal  his guilty verdict in the george floyd's  murder  [Music]  welcome back ex-minnesota police officer  and convicted killer derek shovin  acquires a new attorney after the  minnesota supreme court denied his  request for a public defender  back in 2020 derek showman was convicted  of second and third degree murder and  second-degree manslaughter for the  murder of george floyd he was sentenced  to more than 22 years in prison earlier  this month the minnesota supreme court  denied a public defender for the ex-cop  who plans to appeal his case shoving  said that he had no money and that his  debts greatly outweighed his assets this  left him to represent himself since he  wouldn't be able to cover attorney's  fees his attorney in the previous case  eric nelson was paid through the  minnesota police and peace officers  association legal defense fund in a  recent filing attorney william moram  stated that the law firm of moran  cardinal and erickson pa will appear for  sheriff on chauvin's behalf the  minneapolis police federation says they  are not paying for more man's  representation right now it's unclear  how chovan's newly hired team will be  compensated  matthew what does it mean that schoen  was denied a public defender after his  debts and assets were reviewed  especially since now he's hired an  attorney  well we all know uh brian that that um  we're a defendant is entitled to an  attorney but that doesn't mean he's  entitled to a free attorney if you have  the means to pay for an attorney then  you have to pay for your own counsel  only those who are indigent are entitled  to free representation uh so in this  case as in every case public defender's  office does an initial review they  determine if there's a asset that  there's sufficient assets or income they  refuse uh representation in this  situation that was done it was then  reviewed by the court and the court said  hey  you have the means to hire your own  attorney and you have to do that or  represent yourself  and see how that played out and as of  course some officers have a fund that  they pay into as they as they work and  that's how eric nelson was employed  derrick shoved obviously has a lot of  things on his plate that are taxing his  money no pun intended and that's why he  had to go this route terry what issues  do you think are likely to come up on  appeal  there are so many issues brian that are  going to come up on appeal they've  already talked about there was an abuse  of discretion by the court when there  was a failure to change the venue a  failure to sequester the jury for the  entire trial we know those are two big  issues they also tried to claim that  there was prosecutorial misconduct when  we had the closing arguments and you may  recall that the prosecution said the  defense was shading the truth and that  that was incorrect and wrong and  prosecutorial misconduct so there were a  number of other areas maurice hall also  wasn't allowed so i think that you're  going to see number of  appeal grounds absolutely and thank you  for joining us here on law and crime  daily we'll see you next time as we  discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

